\description{
Construct and manipulate an \link[=effort-class]{effort} object defining historical
fishing effort and spatial structure for a \code{\link[=Fleet]{Fleet()}} object.
}
\details{
An \link[=effort-class]{effort} object defines total fleet fishing activity prior to
spatial allocation. Effort is converted to fishing mortality via gear
efficiency (\code{q}) in the associated \link[=catchability-class]{catchability} object.
\subsection{Effort Array Format}{

When supplied as a numeric array, \code{Effort} must have dimensions \verb{Sim x Year}
with named dimnames. The \code{Sim} dimension may be length 1 (replicated
internally by \code{\link[=PopulateEffort]{PopulateEffort()}}). The \code{Year} dimension must match the
historical years of the OM exactly.

When supplied as a data frame, the format must meet the requirements of
\code{\link[=GenHistEffort]{GenHistEffort()}}, which generates a stochastic \verb{Sim x Year} array during
population. The \code{Units} slot is set to \code{"unitless"} automatically in this
case.
}

\subsection{Spatial Distribution}{

\code{Distribution} is only used for spatial models (\code{nArea > 1}). It must sum
to 1 over the \code{Area} dimension within each simulation and year. The \code{Sim}
and \code{Year} dimensions may be length 1 (replicated internally). When not
specified (default), \code{\link[=PopulateEffort]{PopulateEffort()}} initialises all cells to \code{NA}.

In \code{\link[=Simulate]{Simulate()}} and \code{\link[=Project]{Project()}}, the spatial allocation algorithm only
fills cells that remain  \code{NA}; any non-\code{NA} values supplied in \code{Distribution}
are left unchanged and act as fixed overrides for those
simulation-year-area combinations.
}

\subsection{Spatial Effort Allocation}{

When \code{Distribution} cells are \code{NA}, effort is allocated across the open
areas at each time step as

\deqn{D_{f,k} \propto w_k \, u_{f,k}^{\lambda_f}}

where \eqn{\lambda_f} is \code{Targeting}, \eqn{w_k} is the relative area size
(\code{Spatial@RelativeSize}) for \code{Mode = "Density"} and 1 for
\code{Mode = "Biomass"}, and \eqn{u_{f,k}} is the expected catch per unit of
effort intensity in area \eqn{k}:

\deqn{u_{f,k} = \frac{1}{w_k} \sum_{s} q_{s,f} \sum_a N_{a,k} v_{a,f,k}
  \left[ r_{a,f,k} W^{\mathrm{ret}}_{a,f} + \omega_f (1 - r_{a,f,k})
  W^{\mathrm{sel}}_{a,f} \right] h(F_{s,k})}

with selectivity \eqn{v}, retention \eqn{r}, \code{ReleaseValue}
\eqn{\omega_f}, and depletion discount \eqn{h(F) = (1 - e^{-F}) / F}, the
mean fraction of the biomass available over the time step. \eqn{F_{s,k}} is
the total apical fishing mortality on stock \eqn{s} in area \eqn{k} from all
fleets in the same time step, capped at \code{OM@maxF}, so the allocations of all
fleets are solved jointly.

\code{Targeting} is the elasticity of effort intensity with respect to catch
rate:
\itemize{
\item \code{0}: effort is spread evenly over space (in proportion to \eqn{w_k}),
regardless of where the fish are.
\item \code{1} (default): each area's share of effort is proportional to its
exploitable biomass, discounted for the depletion caused by fishing within
the time step; with light fishing this is its share of exploitable
biomass. For \code{Mode = "Density"}, fishing mortality is then proportional to
exploitable biomass density.
\item Larger values concentrate effort in the areas with the highest catch
rates; as \code{Targeting} increases the allocation approaches the ideal free
distribution, where catch rates are equal across all fished areas.
}

The allocation does not depend on the number of areas: splitting an area
into identical parts leaves the effort and fishing mortality in it
unchanged. Areas with no exploitable biomass receive no effort when
\code{Targeting > 0}. Areas closed to the fleet for every stock (\code{Closure = 0})
receive no effort.
}

\subsection{Effort-to-Trips Conversion and Angler Scaling}{

Bag-limit management procedures require the effort index to be expressed as
absolute angler trip counts. When \code{Units = "trips"} the effort array is
already on the trips scale and no conversion is needed.

When the bag limit is defined per angler (the default), \code{AnglerPerTrip}
provides the mean number of anglers per trip \eqn{A_f(t)}, such that the
fleet-level retention cap is:

\deqn{C^{\text{bag}}_f(t) = B_f \cdot A_f(t) \cdot T_f(t)}

where \eqn{B_f} is the bag limit in fish per angler per trip. The \code{Year}
dimension of \code{AnglerPerTrip} may be length 1 if the value is assumed
constant over time; it is replicated internally to match the full model
time series.

When the bag limit is defined per vessel (boat limit), \code{AnglerPerTrip} is
not used.
}

\subsection{Within-Trip Overdispersion: \code{Theta}}{

\code{Theta} (\eqn{\theta}) parameterises the negative binomial distribution
used by bag-limit management procedures to model within-trip catch counts:

\deqn{n_{f}(t) \sim \text{NegBin}(\mu_{f}(t),\; \theta_{f}(t))}

where \eqn{\mu_{f}(t)} is the mean per-trip catch at time \eqn{t} that
clears all other retention rules (size limits, discard mortality, etc.)
\itemize{
\item i.e. what would be kept absent any bag limit. The bag limit itself
then truncates \eqn{n_{f}(t)} at the cap; \eqn{\mu_{f}(t)} is the
input to that truncation, not its output. The variance of within-trip
catch (before truncation) is:
}

\deqn{\text{Var}(n_{f}) = \mu_{f} + \frac{\mu_{f}^2}{\theta_{f}(t)}}

It determines the fraction of trips that catch at or above the bag
limit at any given mean catch rate, and therefore how strongly the
regulation constrains total retention as stock abundance changes.

Smaller values (e.g. 0.5-2) produce high trip-to-trip variability with
many zero-catch trips and occasional large catches, typical of
recreational marine fisheries. Larger values approach the Poisson
distribution. Only required when a bag-limit management procedure is
active for this fleet.

The \code{Year} dimension of \code{Theta} may be length 1 if the value is assumed
constant over time (the typical case, absent creel data suggesting
otherwise); it is replicated internally to match the full model time
series. A time-varying \code{Theta} can be supplied directly as a \verb{Sim x Year}
array, e.g. to represent a hypothesis that within-trip catch variability
changes with stock depletion.

When trip-level creel data are available, \code{Theta} is estimated by
maximum likelihood fitting of the negative binomial to observed
per-trip counts. In the absence of creel data, \code{Theta} must be assumed
\itemize{
\item e.g. a single plausible value, or a range sampled across simulations
to test management performance against this uncertainty.
}
}

\subsection{Pass-Through Extraction}{

When \code{Effort} is a \link[=fleet-class]{fleet}, \link[=effort-class]{effort}, \link[=hist-class]{hist},
\link[=obs-class]{obs}, \link[=data-class]{data}, \link[=advice-class]{advice}, \link[=advicedata-class]{advicedata},
\link[=imp-class]{imp}, or \link[=mse-class]{mse} object (or a list of \link[=data-class]{data} objects),
\code{Effort()} returns the \code{Effort} slot of that object rather than
constructing a new one. For an \link[=om-class]{om} object, or a list of
\link[=fleet-class]{fleet} objects or a list of them by stock, it returns a list with
the \code{Effort} slot of each fleet, with the same structure as \code{OM@Fleet}.

\code{Effort(x) <- value} replaces the \code{Effort} slot of any of these objects
except \link[=hist-class]{hist} and \link[=mse-class]{mse} objects. For an \link[=om-class]{om} object,
\code{value} is a single \link[=effort-class]{effort} object when the OM has one stock and
one fleet, a list named by fleet when it has one stock and several
fleets, and a list named by stock of lists named by fleet when it has
several stocks. For \link[=hist-class]{hist} and
\link[=mse-class]{mse} objects, the \code{df} argument controls whether a tidy
\code{data.frame} (\code{TRUE}) or the raw array (\code{FALSE}) is returned.
}

\subsection{Stock Targeting Resistance: \code{StockTargetingLambda}}{

When a fleet operates on several stock complexes, a single effort level
usually cannot meet every complex's TAC at once. Effort solving therefore
also adjusts each fleet's stock-targeting weights, subject to a penalty on
how far the mix moves from the previous year. The weight on that penalty is
derived per fleet from the historical variability of its own targeting: a
fleet whose mix has shifted a lot in the past is allowed to shift it again
cheaply, while a fleet with a stable record resists.

The derived weight is normalised so that, at a multiplier of \code{1}, shifting
the targeting mix by a given proportion costs about as much as missing the
TAC by the same proportion.

\code{StockTargetingLambda} scales that derived weight, and is specified per
stock and fleet since it lives on the fleet object:
\itemize{
\item \code{1} (or \code{NULL}) keeps the derived value: targeting shifts and TAC misses
are weighted comparably.
\item \code{0} removes the penalty, so the fleet reallocates targeting freely to hit
its TACs.
\item Values below \code{1} (say \code{0.1}) let targeting move more readily.
\item Values above \code{1} hold the existing mix more strongly; by around \code{100} the
mix is effectively fixed at the previous year's.
}

It has no effect on single-complex operating models, where there is no
competing TAC to reconcile against.
}

\subsection{Attaching to a Fleet}{

An \link[=effort-class]{effort} object can be attached to a \code{\link[=Fleet]{Fleet()}} with
\code{Effort(Fleet) <- MyEffort} and retrieved with \code{Effort(Fleet)}.
}
}
\examples{
## ---- Empty Effort object ----
# An empty Effort object is created by default when constructing a Fleet.
e <- Effort()
e

## ---- Effort from a pre-specified array ----
# A Sim x Year numeric array can be supplied directly.
# Year dimnames must match the historical years of the OM.
hist_years <- 2000:2024
nSim <- 48

effort_array <- array(
  seq(0.2, 1, length.out = length(hist_years)),
  dim = c(1, length(hist_years)),
  dimnames = list(Sim = 1, Year = hist_years)
)

e_array <- Effort(Effort = effort_array, Units = "hours")
e_array_pop <- Populate(e_array, HistYears = hist_years, nSim = nSim, nArea = 3, seed = 42)
Effort(e_array_pop)

## ---- Effort from a data frame (stochastic) ----
# A data frame of control points can be supplied to generate a stochastic
# Sim x Year effort array via GenHistEffort(). Effort is sampled uniformly
# between Lower and Upper at each control year, linearly interpolated to
# annual resolution, perturbed by lognormal process error (CV), and
# normalised to 1 in the terminal year.
effort_df <- data.frame(
  Year  = c(2000, 2008, 2016, 2024),
  Lower = c(0,    0.3,  0.7,  1),
  Upper = c(0,    0.5,  0.9,  1),
  CV    = c(0.1,  0.1,  0.1,  0.1)
)

e_df <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Units = "hours")
e_df_pop <- Populate(e_df, HistYears = hist_years, nSim = nSim, seed = 42)
Effort(e_df_pop) |> head()

## ---- Spatial distribution across 3 areas ----
# Distribution must have dimensions Sim x Year x Area and sum to 1 over the
# Area dimension. Here effort is fixed: 50\% in area 1, 30\% in area 2, and
# 20\% in area 3 across all simulations and years. Supplying non-NA values
# fixes those cells; the internal allocation algorithm only fills NA cells.
dist_array <- array(
  c(0.5, 0.3, 0.2),
  dim      = c(1, 1, 3),
  dimnames = list(Sim = 1, Year = hist_years[1], Area = 1:3)
)

e_dist <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Distribution = dist_array, Units = "hours")
e_dist_pop <- Populate(e_dist, HistYears = hist_years, nSim = nSim, nArea = 3, seed = 42)
Distribution(e_dist_pop)

## ---- Targeting: how strongly effort follows the fish ----
# lambda = 0: effort spread evenly over space (equal F at equal density)
# lambda = 1: effort follows exploitable biomass (default)
# lambda = 3: effort concentrated where catch rates are highest
yr1 <- array(0, dim = c(1, 1), dimnames = list(Sim = 1, Year = hist_years[1]))

e_even  <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Targeting = yr1)
e_high  <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Targeting = yr1 + 3)

## ---- ReleaseValue: catch-and-release fleets ----
# 0 (default): only retained catch attracts effort
# 1: released catch attracts effort as much as retained catch
e_release <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, ReleaseValue = 1)

## ---- Mode: Density vs Biomass ----
# Mode = "Density" (default): F in an area is proportional to effort per unit
# area. Mode = "Biomass": each area is a discrete fishing ground and F is
# proportional to the effort share in that area.
e_density <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Mode = "Density")
e_biomass <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Mode = "Biomass")

## ---- Accessor and replacement functions ----
e <- Effort(Effort = effort_df, Units = "hours")

Mode(e)
Units(e)
Targeting(e)
ReleaseValue(e)
Distribution(e)
Maximum(e)

Mode(e) <- "Biomass"

## ---- Extracting Effort from a Fleet object ----
f <- Fleet(Name = "Trawl", Effort = e)
Effort(f)
}
